/**
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *  GENE KEYS — RADIANCE  (position-specific readings)
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *  The Sun 88° before birth: the body position, and the most somatic of the
 *  four. Vitality, health, energy — what your system actually responds to,
 *  the physical tell when you are running against yourself, what genuinely
 *  restores you, and what other people sense in your presence.
 *
 *  Mastery empowers: it names what the reader's body and energy are good at,
 *  with the reader as subject and actor of nearly every sentence, ending on a
 *  lift rather than a caveat.
 *
 *  Written from the published Shadow/Gift/Siddhi triads alone. Verified for
 *  shape, for distinctness against the universal reading and every other
 *  position, and for cross-position opening clauses — six keys opened too
 *  much like their Evolution or Life's Work counterparts and were recast.
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 */

DGKRoles.register('radiance', {
  "1": {
    "gift": "You run on newness in a way most bodies never manage, waking up genuinely hungry for a day that has not happened yet. Your energy answers to Freshness before it answers to rest — new light, new air, a street you have not walked — and one unfamiliar hour restores you more than a flat weekend off. You recover quickly once anything in the day actually changes, a resilience you have underrated because from the outside it simply looks like restlessness. People catch that first-morning quality in your body and leave your company feeling their own day is less used up.",
    "shadow": "Sameness is what wears you down, not effort. When the week repeats itself without a single new thing in it, your body goes heavy and slack, and the flatness of Entropy shows up as long sleep that leaves you unrested. You reach for stimulation instead of change — screens late, sugar, one more coffee — and the lift lasts an hour before the drop lands harder. Underneath sits a quiet fear that the spark was a phase of youth and has now left you for good, so you stop expecting it and stop looking. The cost is a life that still functions while your appetite for it thins out, and the people close to you feel that dimming before you name it.",
    "invitation": "What did your body last feel curious about? Put one genuinely new thing into tomorrow — a different route, an hour outdoors before anyone speaks to you. You are allowed to treat novelty as nourishment rather than as an indulgence to be earned. When your ordinary days start carrying their own Beauty, what will you stop waiting for?"
  },
  "2": {
    "gift": "You orient by body before you orient by thought, and your physical system settles the moment you are pointed the right way. Your energy tracks direction rather than effort — facing what is genuinely yours, you work long hours and finish steady, while a small task in the wrong direction empties you by noon. You carry an unusually accurate sense of where home is in any situation, an Orientation most people spend years in therapy trying to develop. Others relax near you without knowing why, because a body that is not lost lets everyone else stop scanning for the exit.",
    "shadow": "Being subtly turned the wrong way is what costs you, and it never announces itself loudly. Dislocation arrives as a low background nausea, a sense of being slightly outside your own life, and you push through it because nothing is technically wrong. You start borrowing other people’s direction — their schedule, their appetite, their idea of a good week — and your sleep goes shallow while your body quietly withdraws its cooperation. The belief underneath is that you lost your own compass somewhere and cannot get it back, so asking someone else feels safer than trusting the pull you already feel. What it takes is your appetite: for food, for company, for the day itself.",
    "invitation": "Where in your week does your body feel unmistakably at home? Give that place one protected hour before you give anyone else your morning. You are allowed to change direction on physical evidence alone, without a written justification. When your body and your life face the same way, what does Unity actually feel like from the inside?"
  },
  "3": {
    "gift": "You wake up strongest at the start of things, and your body knows the difference between a beginning and a continuation. Your vitality runs on Innovation — give you a fresh problem and your sleep improves, your appetite returns and you stop needing to be managed. You handle the messy early phase of anything without the nervous drain it causes most people, and you have underrated this because you assume everyone finds unformed situations exciting. Others feel the charge of your presence and find their own first steps easier to take, which is why rooms move faster once you have arrived in them.",
    "shadow": "Long middles are where your health slips. When a project settles into maintenance, Chaos turns inward: your thinking scatters, your days lose shape and you eat and sleep at hours that no longer match anyone. You start several new things at once to get the old feeling back, and the fragmentation costs you more energy than the boredom did. Beneath it is a fear that if you stop generating you will discover there is nothing underneath, so stillness gets treated as a threat rather than as rest. The tell is physical — jittery hands, an inability to sit through a meal, a body that will not switch off even when it is genuinely tired.",
    "invitation": "What is your body doing when the work stops being new? Choose one thing this week to carry past the interesting phase, and rest deliberately while you do it. You are allowed to be still without deciding you have lost your edge. If nothing needed inventing today, where would your Innocence take you instead?"
  },
  "4": {
    "gift": "You digest experience by understanding it, and your whole body settles the moment a confusing thing finally makes sense to you. Your energy responds to clear answers with real physical relief — the jaw loosens, the shoulders drop and sleep comes back the night a question resolves. You hold open questions far longer than most people manage before their nervous system demands a verdict, and this patience of the mind is the strength you most consistently overlook in yourself. People bring you their tangles because your Understanding lands in their body as physical calm, not merely in their head as useful information.",
    "shadow": "Your system runs hot when it cannot resolve something. Intolerance builds as physical tension first — clenched jaw, tight neck, a shortened breath — and you notice the irritability long after your body started carrying it. You reach for a fast answer to end the discomfort, and the premature conclusion locks the tension in rather than releasing it. Underneath is a belief that not knowing is unsafe, so your body treats an open question the way it would treat a threat, and mental restlessness follows you into the night. The cost shows in broken sleep, in a short fuse with people you love, and in headaches that arrive on the days you have thought hardest.",
    "invitation": "Where does your body hold the questions you have not answered? Take a long walk with one unresolved thing and deliberately give it no conclusion at all. You are allowed to leave a question open overnight and still sleep well. When knowing stops being urgent, what does Forgiveness free up in your body?"
  },
  "5": {
    "gift": "You keep time inside your body with unusual accuracy, and your health rests on a steady rhythm far more than on discipline. Your energy answers to regularity — meals, sleep and movement at roughly the same hours — and within days of that steadiness you feel like a different person. You have real Patience in the physical sense, an ability to wait through a slow stretch without your body panicking, which most people simply do not possess. Others slow their breathing near you, because a person visibly keeping their own rhythm gives everyone around them permission to stop rushing theirs.",
    "shadow": "Hurry is the thing that actually damages you. Impatience shows up as a body pushed off its own clock — meals skipped, sleep clipped at both ends, movement crammed into whatever gap is left. You start living for the moment the waiting ends, and the days in between stop registering as life at all, which flattens your appetite and your mood together. The false belief is that your natural pace is too slow for the world you live in, so you override it and call the exhaustion a normal cost of ambition. Weeks of that leave you wired at midnight and hollow at dawn, running on stimulants rather than on the rhythm your system was built around.",
    "invitation": "What time does your body actually want to eat and sleep? Fix one of those hours this week and hold it, even when the day argues loudly with you. You are allowed to move at your own speed while everyone around you rushes. When the waiting stops feeling like waiting, what does Timelessness open up in an ordinary evening?"
  },
  "6": {
    "gift": "You settle a room with your body long before you have said a single conciliatory word to anyone in it. Your energy responds to emotional weather with precision — warmth in your gut when things are clear between people, a physical unease the moment they are not. You recover fast from friction once it is genuinely aired, and this ability to metabolise a hard conversation instead of storing it is rarer than you assume. Your Diplomacy lives in your breathing and your face, so people relax around you and feel their own edges softening without anyone ever naming what changed.",
    "shadow": "Unspoken tension is what makes you ill. Conflict you have swallowed sits in the stomach first, and you lose your appetite days before you admit anything is wrong. You keep the peace by absorbing the discomfort yourself, then find your sleep thinning and your patience gone with people who had nothing to do with it. Beneath this is a belief that your body can hold what the room will not, so you carry other people’s unfinished arguments as if they were yours to digest. The cost arrives as gut trouble, tight breathing and sudden disproportionate flares, and the outburst usually lands on whoever is safest rather than whoever caused it.",
    "invitation": "In which part of you does conflict register before you understand it? Say the uncomfortable thing early just once this week, while it is still small and cheap. You are allowed to stop digesting arguments that were never yours. When your body no longer braces for the next disagreement, what does Peace let you feel instead?"
  },
  "7": {
    "gift": "You steady quickly once you know where a group is actually going, and shared direction feeds your body far more than reassurance ever does. Your energy rises inside a shared purpose — working alongside people who are all moving the same way, you find a level of stamina that solitary effort never gives you. You read the shape of a group’s momentum instinctively, an underrated form of Guidance you assume everyone carries when almost nobody actually does. People stand straighter around you, because a body that is clear about the way forward quietly calms every nervous system in the room.",
    "shadow": "Being pulled in two directions is what drains you. Division inside a group registers in your body as a heaviness in the chest and a fatigue that no amount of sleep touches. You take charge to end the discomfort, then find the effort of holding everyone together eating your evenings, your meals and your rest. Underneath sits a fear that if you stop steering, the whole thing will fracture and it will have been your fault, so you never let the weight down. The cost is a body permanently on duty — shallow sleep, tension across the upper back and a slow loss of the ordinary enjoyment you used to get from being with people.",
    "invitation": "What does your body do when a group loses its direction? Step back from one thing you have been quietly holding together, and notice what happens without you. You are allowed to rest while others find their own way forward. If leading cost you nothing physically, what would Virtue look like in your week?"
  },
  "8": {
    "gift": "You dress, arrange and furnish your life in a way that visibly feeds you rather than merely presenting you. Your body responds to self-expression as directly as it responds to food — wearing what is actually yours, your posture changes and your energy holds all day. You know precisely when something is off by a small margin, in clothing, a room or a sentence, and this fine calibration is a form of Style most people never develop. Others feel more like themselves in your company, because someone comfortably expressed gives everyone else quiet permission to stop blending into the wall.",
    "shadow": "Muting yourself is what costs you physically, and Mediocrity here is no lack of talent but a slow flattening: the safe clothes, the neutral room, the day designed to attract no comment. Your body registers all of that as a dull, low-grade tiredness which never quite lifts. You tell yourself the details are trivial and unimportant, then find yourself listless in surroundings that are technically fine. The fear underneath is that showing your real taste invites judgement, so you shrink the signal and lose the vitality that came with it. Living that way long enough, you stop recognising your own reflection and the fatigue becomes background noise you no longer question.",
    "invitation": "What have you stopped wearing, playing or keeping because it drew attention? Put one of those things back into your week and watch how fast your energy answers. You are allowed to take your own taste seriously as a health practice. Where might your most ordinary days begin showing their own Exquisiteness?"
  },
  "9": {
    "gift": "You build strength in small daily increments, and your body rewards that steadiness far more generously than it ever rewards intensity. Your energy answers to exactly the daily repetition most people find dull — twenty focused minutes, held quietly for weeks, changes you more than any heroic weekend ever manages. You possess real Determination in the physical sense, an ability to keep a small commitment when nothing dramatic is happening, which is far rarer than raw willpower. People find you steadying, because your physical presence carries the quiet confidence of someone who has already proved to themselves that they follow through.",
    "shadow": "Waiting for a big enough reason is what stalls you. Inertia settles in whenever the small step looks too minor to matter, and your body stiffens fast — sleep gets heavier, movement stops entirely and starting again feels absurdly hard. You compensate with sudden intense bursts that leave you sore, discouraged and further from any workable routine than you were before. The belief underneath is that only significant effort counts for anything, so the ten-minute version gets dismissed as a waste of your time. What it costs is momentum itself: strength drains quietly, ordinary tasks start feeling heavy and you begin describing yourself as someone who simply has no discipline.",
    "invitation": "What is the smallest version of the thing you keep postponing? Do that version today, badly if necessary, and again tomorrow. You are allowed to let a tiny action count as real progress. When nothing can knock you off a small daily rhythm, what does Invincibility feel like in your body?"
  },
  "10": {
    "gift": "You inhabit your own body far more easily than almost anyone you know, provided that nobody in the room has asked you to perform. Your vitality answers directly to Naturalness — moving the way you actually move, eating when you are actually hungry, sleeping at the hour the tiredness is real. You hold an unusually direct relationship with your own physical signals, and you have badly underrated it, because you assume everybody hears their own body this clearly. People breathe more easily around you, because someone genuinely at home in their own skin quietly loosens the self-consciousness in everyone else present.",
    "shadow": "Watching yourself is the thing that quietly exhausts you. Self-Obsession here is not vanity but constant self-monitoring — checking how you look, how you sound, how you land — and that surveillance burns energy your body badly needed elsewhere. You start living slightly outside yourself, and the physical signs are unmistakable: shallow breathing, tight posture, and a tiredness that rest never quite clears. Underneath sits a fear that the unedited version of you is not acceptable, so you keep adjusting rather than simply inhabiting. Over time your appetite and your natural ease with movement both fade, and being alone stops feeling restful, because the observer follows you into the empty room.",
    "invitation": "Where do you still watch yourself when nobody else is there? Spend one whole hour this week doing something entirely ordinary with no audience in mind. You are allowed to move through a day without editing yourself. If nothing about you needed adjusting, what would simple Being feel like tonight?"
  },
  "11": {
    "gift": "You brighten physically, visibly, the moment your imagination is given somewhere real to go. Your energy runs on Idealism — a vision you actually believe in puts colour back into your face and gets you out of bed well before the alarm. You keep a rich inner life that genuinely restores you, and unhurried daydreaming works as real physical rest for your system in a way it does for very few other people. Others come away from your company lighter than they arrived, because being near someone who still pictures something better makes their own outlook feel far less closed.",
    "shadow": "A life with nothing luminous in it dulls you fast. Obscurity arrives as a fog you cannot think through — vague heaviness, muddled sleep, days that blur into one another without any single thing being wrong. You retreat further into fantasy to escape the greyness, and the more untethered the picture becomes, the less energy comes back from it. Beneath that is a fear that what you see is not real and could never survive contact with a practical world, so you keep it hidden and it slowly loses its heat. The cost is a body low on light: dim mornings, lost appetite for plans and a growing sense that you are dreaming about a life rather than living inside one.",
    "invitation": "What image of your life still genuinely warms you? Bring one small piece of it into physical reality before this week is over. You are allowed to treat your imagination as nourishment your body actually needs. Where would you feel that inner Light if it were finally visible in your days?"
  },
  "12": {
    "gift": "You taste the difference in everything you take in, and your body keeps a scrupulously honest account of every bit of it. Your energy responds sharply to whatever you consume — food, drink, conversation, media — and you register the effect of a wrong intake within hours rather than within years. You practise Discrimination naturally, sensing what is truly nourishing and what is merely available, a bodily sensitivity that most people spend a whole decade learning to trust. Others find your presence clarifying, because someone unwilling to swallow what does not sit well makes honesty considerably easier for everyone standing nearby.",
    "shadow": "Consuming against yourself is the thing that steadily wears you thin. Vanity here is the pull to take in whatever looks good rather than what suits you: the impressive meal, the flattering company, the words you wanted to hear. Your body pays that bill privately, and you override your own no because refusing looks precious, then sit with the physical aftermath alone. The false belief is that your sensitivity is an inconvenience to other people, so you swallow things you already knew were wrong for you. The cost accumulates in your digestion, in the mood swings that follow certain company, and in a slow distrust of the very instinct that was protecting you all along.",
    "invitation": "What did you take in this week that your body clearly disagreed with? Decline one such thing early, before it ever reaches you next time. You are allowed to be particular about what enters your body and your ears. When nothing you consume is working against you, what does Purity taste like?"
  },
  "13": {
    "gift": "You clock what people are actually carrying, and your body registers the weight of it well before the conversation catches up. Your energy answers to being genuinely listened to in return — a single honest exchange restores you more reliably than a whole quiet weekend by yourself. You have real Discernment about which company feeds you and which company costs you, and you have underrated it because you assume everyone reads a room this accurately. People soften near you and say far more than they meant to, because being properly heard is rare enough to change the way someone breathes.",
    "shadow": "Absorbing other people is what empties you. Discord in a relationship lodges in your chest, and you carry the residue of conversations for days while everyone else has moved on. You keep listening past your own limit, then find yourself flat, tearful or inexplicably sore without connecting it to the hours you spent holding someone else’s difficulty. Underneath sits a belief that turning away would make you cold, so you stay open long after your system has asked to close. The cost is a body that never fully discharges — restless sleep, low appetite, and a slow withdrawal from the people you actually love because you no longer have anything left over for them.",
    "invitation": "Whose feelings are you still carrying in your chest today? Leave one draining conversation early this week, before you have been emptied out. You are allowed to close the door and recover your own signal. When listening no longer drains you, what does Empathy make possible between you and someone you love?"
  },
  "14": {
    "gift": "You carry far more physical capacity than you give yourself credit for, and real work energises you rather than depleting you. Your vitality answers to Competence — doing something you are genuinely good at, your body settles into a rhythm that feels much closer to play than to labour. You generate power for other people simply by being effective near them, which most people experience as inspiration rather than as the resource it truly is. Others take heart around you, because a body that visibly enjoys its own effort makes work look possible again to people who had given up.",
    "shadow": "Settling is what drains you, not overwork. Compromise shows up when you pour your strength into something you do not believe in, and the fatigue that follows is unlike ordinary tiredness — heavy, resentful and unresponsive to sleep. You keep going because the arrangement is reasonable and the money is fine, and your body responds by withdrawing its enthusiasm from everything, including the parts of life you love. The belief underneath is that wanting more from your days is greedy, so you take the safe version and call it maturity. The cost is a slow flattening of appetite and libido, and a growing suspicion that the exhaustion is simply your age.",
    "invitation": "What work leaves you with more energy than it took? Give that thing one real hour this week rather than your leftovers. You are allowed to want your effort to go somewhere you believe in. If your strength were fully spent on what matters, what would Bounteousness look like in your body?"
  },
  "15": {
    "gift": "You bloom in company, and your body works at its best when it is allowed its own natural extremes. Your energy runs in seasons rather than along a flat line — intense flowering stretches, then genuine retreat — and honouring both of them is what keeps your Magnetism alive. You restore faster outdoors than you ever do indoors, and this direct exchange with weather, ground and light is a source of health that most people have lost access to. Others warm to you without ever knowing why, because a person fully in season carries something the whole room actually feels.",
    "shadow": "Forcing yourself into an even output is what deadens you. Dullness arrives when you flatten out your natural extremes in order to seem reliable, and your body answers with a grey, undifferentiated tiredness that no single change fixes. You stay indoors, keep to the schedule and lose the appetite that used to come with being outside and in motion. Underneath is a fear that your intensity is too much for people, so you dampen it and lose your warmth along with it. The cost is a presence that stops carrying any heat: skin, sleep and mood all dim together, and you begin feeling like a background figure in your own life.",
    "invitation": "When were you last outside long enough for your body to change? Take a full hour out in the weather this week, in whatever it happens to be doing. You are allowed to live in seasons instead of a straight line. What starts happening in you when your own Florescence has room to arrive?"
  },
  "16": {
    "gift": "You switch on completely when something genuinely delights you, and your body follows real enthusiasm far more reliably than it follows obligation. Your energy answers to Versatility — several live interests running at once keep you measurably healthier than one dutiful focus ever will. You learn physical skills faster than almost anyone around you, and you have underrated this badly, because picking new things up quickly has always felt normal to you. People catch your enthusiasm the way they catch warmth from a fire, and any room where you are genuinely excited about something starts moving at a different speed.",
    "shadow": "Going through the motions is what makes you unwell. Indifference creeps in when you keep doing something that the interest has already left, and your body drags in ways that no amount of sleep resolves. You persist because stopping would look flaky, and the boredom hardens into a heaviness that spreads into things you genuinely used to love. The belief underneath is that your enthusiasm cannot be trusted as a guide, so you override it and lose the one signal your vitality actually runs on. The cost shows as a sluggish body, a numb week and a growing cynicism that arrives dressed up as being realistic about how life works.",
    "invitation": "What are you still doing after the interest has gone? Drop or properly pause one of those commitments before this month ends. You are allowed to follow enthusiasm as a legitimate measure of health. When your energy goes only where it truly wants to, what does Mastery start to feel like?"
  },
  "17": {
    "gift": "You pace yourself according to what you see coming, and your body settles the moment the next few weeks take a clear shape in your mind. Your far-sightedness is physical as much as mental — you feel the drain of a badly planned month days before it actually arrives. You underrate this early-warning system entirely, treating it as obvious, when most people only learn to read that signal after a serious burnout has already cost them a year. You restore fastest under open horizon: a long walk, a wide view, an unscheduled afternoon, and your energy comes back clean and steady.",
    "shadow": "Opinion is a body state before it is a position, and yours tightens the jaw, shortens the breath, and pulls the eyes into a hard focus. When you argue a point you have already decided, your energy spikes and then falls off a cliff an hour later. The fear underneath is that being wrong about the future means you never saw clearly at all, so you defend the forecast instead of resting. You stop sleeping properly, running scenarios at two in the morning, and appetite goes flat or turns into late, mechanical eating. People near you feel the scanning behind your eyes and brace, and the room gets quietly tiring for everyone in it.",
    "invitation": "Which part of you feels a forecast harden into a verdict? Tonight, put the unfinished question down at ten and sleep on it. You are allowed to not know yet and still rest well. What would your body feel like if seeing clearly cost you nothing at all — the near edge of Omniscience?"
  },
  "18": {
    "gift": "You recover well because your body tells you the truth early, flagging what is off in your food, your sleep, or your week before anyone around you notices. Your integrity runs through the nervous system — a wrong arrangement registers as physical unease long before you are able to explain what is actually wrong with it. You underrate how rare that is, since most people override the same signal for years and only meet it much later as illness. You feel best having improved one small thing — a repaired routine, a corrected habit — and the relief lands in your whole body rather than only in your mind.",
    "shadow": "Judgement lives in the gut with this key, and yours knots when something is wrong that you have not yet said out loud. You scan your own body for faults the way you scan a broken system, and the scanning itself becomes the illness you were looking for. Under that sits a belief that you are only safe once everything has been corrected, which makes ordinary rest feel like negligence. You skip meals while fixing things, sleep badly on unresolved faults, and wake already tense about the day’s imperfections. Others feel the appraisal in your presence, tighten slightly, and the shared energy in the room drops without anyone naming why.",
    "invitation": "How does your body answer in the first second it spots a flaw? This week, leave one small imperfect thing entirely alone and notice how you sleep. You are allowed to be well before everything is fixed. When did wholeness stop needing your correction — the quiet beginning of Perfection?"
  },
  "19": {
    "gift": "You register the emotional weather of a room in your own body, and that sensitivity is real information about what is happening rather than any kind of fragility. Your energy responds hugely to physical warmth — touch, good food shared with people you trust, a bed that feels safe — and it recovers on those terms. You underrate your skin as an instrument, since most people cannot tell a tense room from a settled one until somebody finally says it aloud. You come back to yourself fast outdoors, with animals, or inside a small circle who need nothing from you, and your vitality returns fuller than before.",
    "shadow": "Co-dependence shows up in the body first as a low, constant hum of carrying other people’s states home with you, long after they have gone. When someone near you is unhappy, your own appetite and sleep reorganise around them without your ever having agreed to it. The belief underneath is that closeness must be paid for with your own wellbeing, so you give until you are hollow and call it love. You then crash — flu that arrives the week after the crisis, skin that flares, energy that will not return on any schedule. People sense the need under your kindness and pull back slightly, which is exactly the outcome your body was bracing for.",
    "invitation": "Whose mood are you carrying in your shoulders right now? Take an hour today with nobody to feel for — outside, alone, phone off. You are allowed to be well while someone you love is not. What opens in your body when giving costs you nothing — the far edge of Sacrifice?"
  },
  "20": {
    "gift": "You breathe differently when you are actually here, and your body knows the exact difference between a genuinely present moment and a performed one. Your self-assurance is somatic before it is verbal: the fidgeting stops, your voice drops, and people around you settle without knowing what changed. You underrate how restful you are to be near, assuming everyone drops into the present as easily as you do on a good day, when most people never manage it at all. You refuel in silence and in simple physical doing — cooking, walking, hands busy with something real — and your energy comes back immediate, clean and entirely your own.",
    "shadow": "Superficiality is a body running on the surface of itself, busy all day and never once fully arriving anywhere in it. You fill the gaps with talk, scrolling, and small errands, and by evening you are tired without having done a single thing that fed you in any lasting way. Underneath is a fear that if you stopped moving there would be nothing there, so stillness registers as threat rather than as rest. Your sleep goes shallow, you eat standing up, and your breath stays high in the chest for weeks at a time. People find you charming and somehow hard to reach, and the contact leaves you both a little emptier than before you met.",
    "invitation": "How much of today did you actually inhabit with your body? Do one ordinary thing this evening with no second screen running anywhere in the room. You are allowed to be unimpressive and completely here. What would your energy feel like if simply being here were already enough for a whole day — the near shore of Presence?"
  },
  "21": {
    "gift": "You hold your ground physically, and your body carries a steadiness that other people lean on in a crisis without ever having to ask for it. Your authority is muscular before it is verbal — a straight back, an unhurried pace, hands that do not flutter when the pressure arrives and everyone else is rushing. You underrate how much energy you have for responsibility, taking on weight that would flatten most people inside a fortnight and still sleeping soundly. You restore through hard physical work that you chose yourself — building, digging, lifting, walking distances — and you come back from it stronger rather than merely rested.",
    "shadow": "Control tightens the body from the shoulders down, and yours holds a permanent readiness to take charge of something, anything, before it goes wrong. When you cannot influence an outcome, you grip harder, skip rest, and work straight through the signal your body is sending you. The fear under it is that being held by anyone else means being at their mercy, so you never fully hand over your weight at all. You sleep with your jaw clenched, wake early with the day already organised, and eat like a chore squeezed between two tasks. People feel managed in your presence rather than met, and they stop offering the help that would have restored you.",
    "invitation": "Notice your body when somebody else takes the wheel — what happens? This week, let one thing be handled by another person and go to bed early. You are allowed to be carried. Where would your strength go if you had nothing left to defend — the far horizon of Valour?"
  },
  "22": {
    "gift": "You soften rooms with your body, and your presence carries a warmth that lowers other people’s heart rates even before you have said anything at all to them. Your graciousness is emotional stamina — you hold difficult feeling, yours and other people’s, without your own system tipping into alarm or hurry. You underrate that capacity badly, assuming anybody could sit beside grief for a whole evening and stay genuinely warm the entire way through, when in truth very few people manage it. You refill through beauty, music, good company and long unhurried meals, and your energy returns richer and steadier than plain rest ever gives it on its own.",
    "shadow": "Dishonour lands in the body as shame, and shame in this key goes straight to the chest and to the appetite within hours. When you feel you have behaved below yourself, you withdraw physically, eat badly, and let the whole day lose its shape entirely. You also swing the other way, spending your emotional charge on everyone in the room until there is none left over for you at the end of it. Underneath is a belief that your worth depends on how gracefully you handled the last hard moment, which makes rest conditional on good behaviour. People feel the effort behind your warmth on those days, and the room turns careful and quiet around you.",
    "invitation": "Where does shame land in your body first? When it does, eat something good and go outside instead of withdrawing to a dark room. You are allowed to be tired and still be warm. What would your body feel like if your worth were never once in question — the beginning of Grace?"
  },
  "23": {
    "gift": "You shed what is unnecessary by instinct, and your body relaxes the moment your surroundings and your schedule get genuinely simpler and quieter. Your simplicity is a physiological requirement rather than a preference: few inputs, plain food, quiet hours, and your energy becomes remarkable to people who assume they need more. You underrate how clearly you see the one thing that matters, when most people need weeks to strip a problem that far down, and you do it in an afternoon. You come back to life in solitude, and you return from it with far more to give than the busy version of you ever had to offer.",
    "shadow": "Complexity is a nervous system with too many tabs open, and yours shows it in scattered sleep and a mind still running hard at midnight. When your life carries too many threads, you fragment — half-finished meals, half-finished days, and a tiredness that no amount of sleep seems to clear. You explain yourself in longer and longer loops, and the explaining burns exactly the energy you needed for living the day. The fear underneath is that if you said the simple thing plainly, nobody would find it worth very much at all. People sense the static around you and give you room, which leaves you more isolated and more tangled than you were before.",
    "invitation": "What could you take out of this week without missing it even slightly? Remove one commitment today and keep the freed hour completely empty. You are allowed to want less than you have. What clears in your body when one plain thing is already enough — the near edge of Quintessence?"
  },
  "24": {
    "gift": "You sleep and wake to your own rhythm, and your body produces its best thinking out of genuine rest rather than out of pressure or a deadline. Your invention arrives on the far side of silence — a walk, a nap, an empty morning — and the returning thought lands whole and already finished. You underrate this cycle, which most people fight all their lives and lose, and it is the single most efficient use of energy you have, bar none. You feel strongest when you protect blank time as fiercely as work time, and the rest of your health follows that one decision about your calendar.",
    "shadow": "Addiction here is a loop the body runs to avoid an empty moment, and it begins long before anything looks like a problem to anyone. The same thought returns at night, and you feed it with a screen, a drink, or another hour of not going to sleep at all. Under it sits a fear that silence means the ideas have stopped, so you keep the mind noisy to prove you are still alive. Your mornings get heavier, your appetite becomes habit rather than hunger, and the rhythm you actually run on quietly breaks underneath you. People feel you half-present, circling something you will not put down, and the contact tires them out.",
    "invitation": "Why is the noise still on? Tonight, put the screen down an hour before bed and let the quiet be boring. You are allowed to have no ideas for a while. What arrives in you when nothing at all is being filled — the doorway into Silence?"
  },
  "25": {
    "gift": "You absorb life directly, and your body opens to warmth, weather, contact and ordinary experience with remarkably little defence in the way of any of it. Your acceptance is physical rather than philosophical: you take a hard day into your system and let it move through you instead of storing it in your shoulders for a month. You underrate how healing your presence is, since people unclench near you without your having done anything deliberate at all, which almost nobody else manages. You come alive in nature and in open-hearted company, and your vitality is at its largest when your heart is unguarded and the day is simple.",
    "shadow": "Constriction is the body closing itself after being hurt, and yours contracts in the chest and stays shut long past the actual danger has gone. When you are guarded, your breath shortens, your circulation runs cool, and small ordinary things exhaust you out of all proportion to their size. The belief underneath is that opening again guarantees another wound, so you ration your own warmth carefully and call it plain prudence. You get ill in the closed seasons — recurring infections, tight shoulders, a sleep that never quite restores you to full. People experience you as pleasant and armoured, and that distance keeps out exactly the contact that would have healed you fastest.",
    "invitation": "Is there somewhere in your chest still closed? Let one person closer than is comfortable this week and notice how you sleep afterwards. You are allowed to open before you feel safe. What happens in your body when nothing is being kept out — the first taste of Universal Love?"
  },
  "26": {
    "gift": "You spend your energy skilfully, knowing by instinct how much to spend on a given day and how much to hold in reserve for tomorrow, and you are rarely wrong. Your artfulness includes the body itself — you rest strategically, recover quickly, and go genuinely hard on the days when something actually matters to you. You underrate that timing sense completely, since athletes and performers train for years to acquire it and still get it wrong when the stakes are high. You feel strongest when your effort is honest and well-paced, and your health rewards that honesty almost immediately with sleep that works and mornings that start easily.",
    "shadow": "Pride runs the body well past its own limit in order to protect an image of being unstoppable to everybody watching. You push through the tired week, sell yourself the comfortable story that you are fine, and pay the whole bill a fortnight later in one go. The fear underneath is that visible weakness would cost you the standing you have built, so you hide the crash from everyone who knows you. You over-caffeinate, undersleep, and market your own stamina to yourself the way you market everything else you do. People sense the performance in your energy, and that small dishonesty puts distance between you and the real support you actually wanted.",
    "invitation": "Which tiredness are you pretending not to feel right now? Say the true version of how you are doing to one person today. You are allowed to be seen at low power. Where does your energy go when there is no image left to maintain at all — the quiet of Invisibility?"
  },
  "27": {
    "gift": "You feed people, and your own vitality rises measurably when you are caring for something living and watching it visibly thrive under your hands. Your altruism is nutritional in both directions: you eat properly when you cook for others, and you sleep properly when the people you love are safe and fed. You underrate how accurately you read what a body needs — yours, a child’s, an animal’s — long before anyone involved puts it into words, and most people never learn to read it. You are at your strongest inside a household or a team you nourish, and that role restores you rather than draining you.",
    "shadow": "Selfishness in this key rarely looks selfish from outside — it looks like feeding everybody else and forgetting entirely to feed yourself anything at all. You skip your own meals, delay your own appointments, and treat your body as the one system in your life that is able to wait indefinitely without complaint. Then the resentment arrives without warning, and with it the swing into hoarding your energy from everyone who asks anything at all of you. Under it sits a belief that your needs are less legitimate than theirs, which quietly licenses years of ordinary self-neglect. People feel the ledger running quietly under your generosity, and the care stops nourishing either one of you.",
    "invitation": "When did you last feed yourself as well as you feed everybody else? Cook one proper meal today for yourself alone and eat it sitting down. You are allowed to take before you give. What flows through you when caring costs you nothing at all — the ease of Selflessness?"
  },
  "28": {
    "gift": "You throw yourself in completely, and your body is measurably happier at full commitment than at any careful, sensible half-measure of the same thing, and it always has been. Your totality shows as physical courage — you go into the deep end of an experience and come back out more alive than you went in, every single time. You underrate your appetite for risk and hardship, which most people spend an entire lifetime carefully arranging their ordinary days to avoid entirely. You recover best after real physical exertion, and the tiredness that follows a wholehearted day is the good kind that sleeps well and wakes clear.",
    "shadow": "Purposelessness drains the body faster than overwork ever does, and yours goes grey when nothing you are doing feels worth the doing at all. Without a stake in something real, you lose the will to move at all, eat listlessly, and sleep far longer than your body actually needs to. You then chase intensity for its own sake — extremes, risk, late nights — and confuse being wired with being genuinely alive for a while. The fear underneath is that life has no meaning and that yours is running out unspent on anything real. People feel either the flatness or the recklessness, and neither state lets them close enough to help you out of it.",
    "invitation": "Which thing gets less than everything from you right now? Choose one thing this week and go in with your body, not only your intentions about it. You are allowed to be tired from something wholehearted. What is left of you when you hold nothing at all back — the current of Immortality?"
  },
  "29": {
    "gift": "You commit your whole physical self behind a yes, and your energy becomes enormous the moment you have genuinely said yes to something worth saying yes to at all, and it stays. Your commitment is physical stamina as much as intention — you go the distance on work that would exhaust people who never fully decided to begin in the first place. You underrate the sheer power of that whole-bodied yes, which most people water down into a maybe long before they have even properly started. You rest properly only once the leap has been made, and that rest is deep, honest and complete in a way that light, hedging sleepers never know.",
    "shadow": "Half-heartedness leaves the body in a permanent idle — engaged enough to be tired, never engaged enough to feel satisfied by any of it. You say yes with your mouth and no with your energy, and the mismatch surfaces as a fatigue nobody around you can account for. You overcommit, then run on obligation, and your sleep gets short and thin in the weeks before you finally withdraw from it. The belief underneath is that a whole yes would trap you, so you keep an exit open and pay for it in vitality. People feel your reservation, and they stop relying on you at exactly the moment you most wanted to be relied on.",
    "invitation": "Where are you saying yes with an exit quietly held open? Close one of those doors today and watch carefully what your energy does by the evening. You are allowed to want something completely. When did whole commitment start feeling like freedom inside your body — the warmth of Devotion?"
  },
  "30": {
    "gift": "You burn beautifully when your desire is clean, and your body runs on genuine enthusiasm far more efficiently than it ever runs on discipline or sheer willpower, and always has. Your lightness is a physical state rather than an attitude — you feel it first in the chest, and it changes how the whole day metabolises around it. You underrate how contagious that state is, since people leave your company carrying more energy than they arrived with, and rarely know quite why it happened. You restore through pleasure taken without any guilt attached: warmth, laughter, appetite, and the small ordinary delights of an otherwise unremarkable afternoon at home.",
    "shadow": "Desire without lightness becomes a fire that eats its own fuel, and the body quietly pays the heating bill for all of it. You want intensely, chase the wanted thing well past exhaustion, and feel oddly flat the moment you finally have it in your hands at last. The craving moves through food, sex, work and stimulation, and none of it lands anywhere near where the real hunger actually is. Underneath is a belief that the next thing will finally settle you, which keeps rest permanently postponed to some later date that never comes. People feel the heat and the restlessness, and being near you becomes exciting and quietly tiring at the same time.",
    "invitation": "Your body warned you weeks ago — about what you about weeks ago? Take one pleasure today without earning it first and without apologising to anyone. You are allowed to enjoy this without a reason. What burns in you when the wanting itself already feels good — the opening into Rapture?"
  },
  "31": {
    "gift": "You stand in a way that people follow without being asked, and your physical presence carries authority in a room long before you have said a single word to anyone. Your leadership is voiced through the body rather than the argument — the breath behind the sentence is what actually makes a room turn and listen to you properly. You underrate how completely your own physical state sets the temperature for everybody else in the building, which almost nobody notices in themselves. You are restored by speaking honestly and being properly heard, and your energy rises for hours after you have told a plain, difficult truth out loud.",
    "shadow": "Arrogance is a body braced to be right, and bracing that long shows up in the neck, the throat and the quality of your sleep. When you speak from position rather than from truth, your voice tightens and the energy afterwards drops away hard. You take on more than the role actually requires, refuse the help that is offered, and your health becomes the invisible cost of looking permanently capable. The fear underneath is that without the leading voice you would not matter to anyone, so you never step down long enough to actually recover. People start agreeing rather than engaging, and the loneliness of that drains you far more than the workload itself ever did.",
    "invitation": "Whose voice have you not yet let into the room? Ask one person what they actually think and stay completely quiet while they answer. You are allowed to lead from not knowing the answer. Where does your energy go when you stop holding the position at all — the relief of Humility?"
  },
  "32": {
    "gift": "You conserve what genuinely deserves keeping, and your body thrives on continuity — the same bed, familiar rhythms, and food you already know suits your own system. Your preservation instinct is real physical wisdom rather than caution: you sense what will last, and you pace your own energy accordingly, year after year without noticing. You underrate your endurance badly, since it outlasts faster people by decades precisely because you never spend what you cannot easily replace in a hurry. You are strongest inside routines you built for yourself, and that quiet steadiness makes you the healthiest person in most rooms you walk into at forty and at seventy.",
    "shadow": "Fear of failure sits in the stomach with this key, and it tightens every time something you have built starts to look fragile or temporary. You hold on well past the point of usefulness, and the holding costs far more of your energy than the letting go ever would have cost. You lose whole nights of sleep protecting what might be lost, and that vigilance ages you faster than any actual loss ever could. Underneath is a belief that if this ends then you end along with it, so rest feels like abandoning your post entirely. People feel the anxiety inside your carefulness, and they hesitate to bring you anything new or unfinished.",
    "invitation": "Which thing are you protecting that no longer needs you to hold it? Let one small thing end this week and sleep on the space it leaves behind. You are allowed to rest while something changes. What steadies in your body when nothing at all needs preserving any more — the depth of Veneration?"
  },
  "33": {
    "gift": "You notice the exact moment your body starts running on autopilot, and that noticing is itself the thing that restores you. Your energy answers to pauses far more than to caffeine — a few minutes of genuine stillness resets you the way a nap resets other people. You recover from hard weeks by withdrawing early rather than pushing through, and you have never given yourself proper credit for that instinct. Mindfulness in your case is not a practice bolted on afterwards; it is how your nervous system already prefers to breathe, and people near you slow down without being asked.",
    "shadow": "Forgetting arrives in your body as a blur: whole days pass and you cannot recall eating, standing up, or how you got quite so tired. Under pressure you speed up, skipping meals and skimming sleep, and the fatigue lands days later as a heavy flatness you cannot explain to anyone. You retreat when overloaded, then let the retreat stretch until it becomes hiding, and the hiding drains more than the crowd ever did. Underneath sits a quiet belief that your inner life only counts once the work is finished, so rest gets postponed indefinitely. The physical tell is a tight jaw, shallow breath, and a mind running three steps ahead of your own feet.",
    "invitation": "How did your body feel an hour ago? Set one alarm today that asks nothing of you except two minutes of breathing. You are allowed to rest before you have earned it. If stillness stopped being a reward and became your baseline, what Revelation might your own body hand you?"
  },
  "34": {
    "gift": "You possess more physical power than the people around you realise, and your body thrives on being used hard rather than carefully spared. Movement is your medicine — a hard walk, a heavy lift, a long day outdoors returns your mood faster and more completely than any conversation does. You recover quickly once you actually stop, sleeping deeply and waking clear in a way many people spend years of effort trying to learn. Your Strength shows in your posture and your stride, and a room registers it before you have said a single word, which quietly steadies everyone in it who is uncertain.",
    "shadow": "Force is the version of this energy that refuses to wait for the right moment, or for your own body to be ready. You push through tiredness because stopping feels like weakness, and the bill arrives as inflammation, injury, or a night your body flatly refuses to shut down. With no physical outlet the power turns inward and comes out as irritability, clenched shoulders, and a short fuse with people who have done nothing at all. An old belief runs underneath that resting makes you dispensable, so you keep proving the opposite until something tears. Frustration is the reliable early signal, and it shows up long before the exhaustion you eventually admit to out loud.",
    "invitation": "Locate the pressure honestly — where is it sitting right now, honestly? Move it today, properly, until you sweat and your breath is loud enough to hear. You are allowed to stop while you still have something left. When your power waits for its own timing instead of forcing it, what Majesty settles into your ordinary standing?"
  },
  "35": {
    "gift": "You quicken fastest when something is new, and novelty does more for your day-to-day vitality than any fixed routine ever will. Your body registers a change of scene as genuine rest — a different city, a different route home, and the tiredness of a whole flat month lifts within hours. You throw yourself into things at full intensity and then recover completely within days, a cycle most people could not survive twice. That appetite for Adventure is not restlessness to be managed; it is your actual fuel line, and people feel the charge of it from the moment you walk in the door.",
    "shadow": "Hunger runs this body whenever nothing new sits anywhere on the horizon. You reach for the next experience the way others reach for sugar, and the crash afterwards is real: flat mood, poor sleep, a low restlessness with no target and no obvious cure. Long stretches of sameness show up physically as heaviness in your limbs and a strange, bored fatigue that ordinary rest does not touch. Underneath all of it is a fear that stillness equals stagnation, so you keep the calendar permanently full and never let your system finish a single cycle. The tell is the twitch — a jiggling leg, a phone opened for the fifth time in ten unremarkable minutes.",
    "invitation": "Underneath the obvious answer, what are you hungry for today, underneath the obvious answer? Change one small physical thing — a new walking route, a meal you have never once cooked before. You are allowed to be still without calling it stuck. If your appetite stopped chasing and started receiving, where would Boundlessness find you sitting?"
  },
  "36": {
    "gift": "You feel emotional weather in your body before your mind has named it, and that early warning keeps you honest about what you actually need that day. Your energy moves in waves, and you have learned to ride the low days rather than fight them, which is a skill most people never develop at all. You recover from hard experience faster than your history would predict, because you let each feeling move all the way through your body. Your Humanity shows up as warmth others physically relax into, and people tell you things at your kitchen table that they have told nobody else alive.",
    "shadow": "Turbulence arrives as churn: a stomach that knots the night before difficult days, and sleep that shatters at three in the morning. You ride a crisis well and then flatten afterwards, and the flat stretch afterwards gets treated as failure rather than the recovery it plainly is. When feeling gets too big you numb it with food, screens, or a deliberately busy week, and the numbing costs more energy than the emotion would have. Beneath it sits a belief that your intensity makes you too much for people, so you swallow it and your body carries what your mouth did not. The result is a chest that stays tight for weeks and a jaw that aches by Friday.",
    "invitation": "Name what your body still holds from this week? Say one true feeling out loud today, to someone you trust, or simply to the empty room. You are allowed to have a low day without repairing it. When your turbulence is simply weather passing through, what Compassion becomes possible in your own skin?"
  },
  "37": {
    "gift": "You gather real physical energy from belonging, and a good unhurried meal with your people restores you far more than a weekend alone ever does. Your body softens in company and stiffens in isolation, and you read that signal far more accurately than you have ever given yourself credit for. You give warmth through touch and presence — a hand on a shoulder settles a room in a way most people could not manage with an entire speech. Equality is how your whole system regulates: among people who treat you as one of them, your appetite, your sleep, and your mood all come back on their own.",
    "shadow": "Weakness here is the slow leak of giving past your own limit. You keep feeding, hosting, and holding a whole family together long after your own energy has gone, and it shows as headaches, a short temper, and a body that catches every bug going around. When belonging feels shaky you appease, agreeing to things your gut has already refused, and the swallowed no sits in your stomach for days. The old fear is that asking for anything at all will get you dropped, so you make yourself endlessly easy and pay for it in exhaustion. You notice it most on Sunday nights, when the accumulated tiredness has nowhere left at all to hide.",
    "invitation": "Who actually fed you this week, in the plainest sense of the word? Ask one person for something small, specific and slightly inconvenient today. You are allowed to need other people out loud. If your warmth flowed in both directions instead of only one, what Tenderness would your tired body finally get to rest inside?"
  },
  "38": {
    "gift": "You outlast almost everyone in the room, and your body is genuinely built for the long stretch rather than for the short sprint. You find your energy through resistance — a hard climb, a difficult project, a fight genuinely worth having wakes you up where comfort quietly puts you to sleep. You recover through further physical effort rather than through lying down, which is the exact opposite of the standard advice and entirely correct for you. Your Perseverance registers in your physical presence as plain, visible steadiness, and people under real pressure stand closer to you without ever knowing why.",
    "shadow": "Struggle turns into a standing habit long after the fight itself is over. You pick battles your body does not need, and the constant low-grade combat shows as jaw tension, shallow breathing, and a sleep that never fully lets go of you. With no worthy opponent you invent one — an argument, an impossible deadline — and burn good vitality on friction that returns you nothing at all. Underneath is the belief that anything easy cannot be real, so you refuse the smooth path and arrive tired at places you could have walked to. The cost lands squarely in your immune system, generally in the week just after you finally stop.",
    "invitation": "Which fight are you actually in right now, this week? Drop one battle today that you never once chose on purpose. You are allowed to take the easy route this once. When your fighting is only for what matters to you, what Honour comes to live in your body at rest?"
  },
  "39": {
    "gift": "You surge on raw charge, and your body comes alive around noise, music, movement, and people who meet your intensity head on. You break your own stuck states physically — a hard run, a dance, a raised voice — faster than any amount of thinking about them would ever do it. You sense the flatness in a room long before anyone has spoken, and your presence alone shifts it, which almost nobody else manages to do deliberately. Your Dynamism is a genuine and reliable source of vitality, and when you stop suppressing it your sleep, your appetite, and your mood all fall back into line.",
    "shadow": "Provocation is what this energy does when it has nowhere useful at all to go. You needle the people closest to you, get the reaction, and then carry the adrenaline around in your body for hours afterwards. Sleep is the first casualty: the system stays switched on, the mind circles, and morning arrives with the tank already half empty. When you damp yourself down to keep the peace, the charge does not vanish — it settles as tension in the gut and a dull, jumpy irritation. Beneath both extremes runs an old fear that calm means nothing is happening, so you keep striking sparks simply to prove that you are alive.",
    "invitation": "Where is your charge actually going to go this evening, once the day is done? Burn it off physically today, well before it reaches anyone you love. You are allowed to be this intense without apologising for it. If your energy pushed against real limits instead of people, what Liberation would your body already be tasting?"
  },
  "40": {
    "gift": "You endure loads that would simply flatten most people you know, and your body knows exactly how much it has left in reserve whenever you listen. You restore yourself through plain solitude — a closed door and a few uninterrupted hours do more for you than a whole week of good company. You have a clean, early instinct for exactly when to say no, and honouring that instinct returns your energy within about a day. Your Resolve is physical rather than mental, and people feel the reliability in your presence long before you have promised them a single thing.",
    "shadow": "Exhaustion is the specific price you pay for saying yes well past your own limit. You commit to things from a strong moment, then meet the obligation from a completely empty one, and the body answers with heaviness, resentment, and a sleep that does not repair anything. Isolation becomes the only rest you will allow yourself, and it stretches from a single night into whole weeks until people wonder what they did wrong. Underneath sits an old arithmetic in which the load you shoulder counts as proof of your value, so the total goes permanently unchecked. The physical tell is your shoulders creeping upward, and a long sigh you let out without ever noticing it.",
    "invitation": "How much do you actually have left in you today, truthfully? Refuse one thing outright, and out loud, before this day is over. You are allowed to rest long before you collapse. When your yes comes only from real, present strength, what Divine Will begins moving quietly through your most ordinary energy?"
  },
  "41": {
    "gift": "You forecast the weather change before there is any evidence, and your body starts preparing for it while everyone around you is still comfortable. New beginnings feed you — the first week of anything gives you more usable energy than the ten weeks that follow it. You carry a low, constant hum of Anticipation that keeps you creative, and it is a genuine engine rather than the nervousness you have long mistaken it for. You settle quickly once something real starts, eating and sleeping properly again, and people find your excitement contagious in a way that lifts entire rooms with it.",
    "shadow": "Fantasy burns real fuel on futures that never once arrive. You live weeks ahead in your imagination, and the body pays with a fluttering stomach, shallow sleep, and a hunger that food does not settle. When the anticipation has no outlet you swing between wired and flat, unable to start anything because the imagined version is already better than anything you could build. There is a fear here that ordinary life is not enough, so you keep the daydream running and let the actual day go unlived. The clearest tell is eating for stimulation rather than for hunger, generally late at night when the built-up pressure has nowhere else to go.",
    "invitation": "What exactly have you been imagining all week long instead of starting? Begin one small, real, unglamorous thing within the next hour. You are allowed to want more than you currently have. When your longing meets the present instead of outrunning it, what Emanation begins moving through your body first?"
  },
  "42": {
    "gift": "You let go bodily once you have accepted that they are genuinely over, and your body recovers from endings noticeably faster than your friends’ bodies do. Rest comes easily to you whenever you let a cycle properly finish, and your sleep deepens on the very night you stop forcing a particular outcome. You keep your energy through Detachment, staying fully involved without being gripped, which is something other people spend decades of meditation practice trying to reach. You have a natural physical sense of when something has run its course, and honouring that sense returns both your appetite and your humour within a few days.",
    "shadow": "Expectation is the thing that tightens this particular body. You decide well in advance how a day, a meal, or a particular person ought to go, and the mismatch lands as a headache and a mood you cannot shift for hours. When something ends before you were ready you hold on physically — clenched hands, disturbed sleep, and a chest that will not open. The fear underneath all of it is that letting go means it never really mattered, so you grip the finished thing and drag its weight into the following week. Endings that you refuse to mark show up months later as a tiredness with no obvious source at all.",
    "invitation": "Which ended thing is still in both hands that has already quietly ended? Mark one ending today, however small or silly the ritual. You are allowed to let something simply be over. If each finish were a doorway rather than a loss, what Celebration would your body be free to feel?"
  },
  "43": {
    "gift": "You catch insights in your body well before the words for them arrive, and your energy is unmistakably better on the days when you follow them. Quiet feeds you, and solitude with long unbroken hours restores you the way good company restores other people. You think best while moving and entirely alone, and long walks alone return more to you than a full night of sleep does in some weeks. Your Insight arrives whole, in a single flash, and the physical relief that follows a good one is real — people notice you visibly lighten the moment something has landed.",
    "shadow": "Deafness begins for you as sheer noise. In a crowded, chattering environment your whole system tightens, your hearing narrows, and by evening you are wrung out for no reason that you can name. You override your own inner timing in order to match other people’s pace, and the mismatch shows as headaches, digestive trouble, and a mind that will not switch off at night. When you finally speak from a place nobody can follow, the rejection lands physically in you and you withdraw much further than the situation ever warranted. Beneath it sits an old fear of being fundamentally unreachable, which makes constant noise feel safer than silence even as it drains you.",
    "invitation": "How much real, uninterrupted silence did you actually get today, all told? Take thirty minutes entirely alone today, with no input of any kind. You are allowed to leave the loud room early, without explaining. When your own timing sets the pace, what Epiphany is your body already waiting there to deliver?"
  },
  "44": {
    "gift": "You sync with other bodies remarkably quickly, and the right group of people raises your energy more reliably than either good food or a long sleep. You read who is well and who is quietly struggling from the smallest physical cues, a whole-body scan that most other people cannot run at all. You work best standing shoulder to shoulder with others, and shared physical effort leaves you charged where solo effort leaves you strangely flat. Teamwork restores you in a literal, measurable, physical way, and the people you gather around you feel measurably healthier in your company than they ever do apart.",
    "shadow": "Interference is other people’s states arriving inside your own body as though they were yours all along. You leave a difficult meeting carrying a stomach ache that belongs to somebody else, and it takes a full day to clear. Old family patterns surface in you as physical wariness — a held breath around certain personalities long before they have actually done anything at all. When you stay in the wrong company out of sheer loyalty, the drain is severe: broken sleep, low appetite, and a persistent heaviness. Underneath is a fear that choosing your own people makes you disloyal, so you keep sitting in rooms that quietly take your health.",
    "invitation": "Whose energy are you carrying around in your body right now? Spend an unhurried hour today with one person who reliably leaves you lighter. You are allowed to choose your company very carefully. When you sit only among people your body trusts, what Synarchy starts running through the whole group?"
  },
  "45": {
    "gift": "You blend easily into almost any group’s natural rhythm, and eating, working, or resting alongside other people feeds you far more than solitude ever does. Your body is at its very happiest as part of something shared — a shared table, a team, a room of people breathing at the same pace. You keep your vitality by giving other people a place to belong, and hosting other people genuinely restores you rather than draining anything out of you. Your Synergy is felt physically by everyone present, and you badly underrate how much a whole group’s health depends on your simply being in the room.",
    "shadow": "Dominance creeps in the moment the group stops moving at your preferred pace. You tighten up, take over everything, and the effort of carrying everybody lands in your neck, your back, and a sleep that never quite finishes. When you have no circle around you at all, isolation hits you harder than it hits most people, showing up as low appetite and a hollow flatness. There is a fear that without your control the whole thing quietly falls apart, so you grip every arrangement and hand yourself all of the exhaustion. The clearest tell is eating fast and alone, standing up at the counter, while telling yourself there is no time.",
    "invitation": "Who did you last share a real, unhurried, sit-down meal with? Sit down and eat with someone this week, slowly and properly unhurried. You are allowed to be held by the very group you hold. If leading meant simply belonging, what Communion would your body finally get to relax into?"
  },
  "46": {
    "gift": "You lighten a room by being in it, and your own body is at its healthiest when you are enjoying something with no purpose attached to it. Physical pleasure genuinely restores you — good food, warm water, sunlight, touch — and you recover through your senses far quicker than through any amount of analysis. You land back in your body faster than most people after stress, reliably within an hour of doing something you actually like. Delight is your medicine and your barometer: when it goes missing your whole health slips, and when it comes back everything else comes back with it.",
    "shadow": "Seriousness is what this body does whenever pleasure keeps getting postponed. You treat rest as something that has to be earned first, push straight through the good weather, and end up with a stiffness and heaviness that no amount of discipline shifts. When you disconnect from your own senses, food becomes mere fuel, touch becomes awkward, and your energy quietly drops away without any clear cause. Beneath it is an old suspicion that enjoying yourself is frivolous while any work is still outstanding, and there is always, without exception, work outstanding. The tell is a body you talk about rather than live inside, described only in problems, numbers and measurements.",
    "invitation": "How long since you simply enjoyed being in your own body? Do one purely pleasurable physical thing today, with no justification at all. You are allowed to have fun long before the work is finished. What Ecstasy is waiting in an ordinary afternoon you have been too busy to notice?"
  },
  "47": {
    "gift": "You metabolise hard experience through your body, and you come out of dark stretches carrying more vitality than you went into them with. Your energy moves in cycles, and you know the low phase is processing rather than decline, which spares you the extra panic that other people pile on top of it. You transform mood physically — sleep, water, a change of light — and that approach works for you where talking about it does not. Transmutation is no theory in your case: your system genuinely converts difficulty into fuel, and people sense that depth in your physical presence.",
    "shadow": "Oppression feels like a heavy lid coming slowly down over everything. Your thinking loops, your chest tightens, and the whole body slows into a fog that feels permanent for as long as it lasts. You fight the low phase instead of letting it simply run, and the resistance costs you far more energy than the mood itself ever would. Underneath is a fear that this time the dark stretch will simply not end, so you brace hard against it and turn a passing weather system into weeks. The physical signs are early and consistent — shallow breathing, a hunched posture, and a sudden loss of interest in the food you normally love.",
    "invitation": "How is your breathing running at this very moment? Take ten slow breaths and let your shoulders drop all the way. You are allowed to be in the dark for a good while. When the low tide is trusted rather than fought, what Transfiguration is your body quietly rehearsing?"
  },
  "48": {
    "gift": "You improvise your way back to health with whatever happens to be available, and your instincts about what your own body needs next are unusually accurate. You draw your energy from depth rather than from breadth — one absorbing subject or skill restores you where sheer variety scatters most other people. You know precisely when to stop and when to carry on, a self-reading that most people never develop past plain guessing. Your Resourcefulness shows up physically as a steady calm that other people borrow, and in a real crisis your body settles down while everyone else’s speeds right up.",
    "shadow": "Inadequacy shows up in your body first of all as physical hesitation. You hold back from the very thing you are already ready for, and the held-back energy turns into a restless tension that quietly ruins your sleep. Over-preparation is that same fear wearing a useful mask: you research and refine until the effort has exhausted you before you have even begun. When the doubt is loudest your appetite goes strange, either forgotten entirely or used to fill the gap where the confidence used to be. Beneath it all sits an old sense that you are missing some basic thing everyone else was handed, which no amount of competence has ever silenced.",
    "invitation": "What exactly is it you are waiting to feel ready for? Do one unprepared thing today, badly and entirely on purpose, without rehearsing it. You are allowed to start well before you feel adequate. If your own depth were enough as it stands, what Wisdom would your body already be living from?"
  },
  "49": {
    "gift": "You move in cycles rather than one flat line, and your body announces plainly when a chapter of energy has closed and a new one wants room. Your vitality returns fastest when you let the outer details change with you — new sleep hours, new food, new movement, a whole redrawn daily shape. You register the emotional temperature of a room straight through your skin, a sensitivity most people spend years of practice trying to develop deliberately. That instinct for personal revolution keeps your body adaptable and young, because you rebuild yourself at the threshold instead of grinding along on fuel that ran out months ago.",
    "shadow": "Reaction moves through the nervous system before it ever reaches thought, and yours fires early and hard. When something crosses a line you have never spoken aloud, your chest tightens, your jaw locks, and the whole day’s energy burns off in ten seconds. The cost arrives later as broken sleep, a shrunken appetite, and a body still braced for a fight that finished hours ago. Underneath sits an old belief that you had better catch the betrayal before it lands, so your system never fully stands down and rest stays shallow. The opposite pattern costs just as much: you swallow the reaction, stay pleasant, and carry the unspent charge as headaches, gut trouble, or flat grey exhaustion.",
    "invitation": "What has your body already finished with that your calendar still insists on? Change one physical routine this week — the hour you sleep, the food you buy, the route you walk. You are allowed to end something before you can fully justify ending it. When your endings get clean, what rebirth becomes possible in a body that finally stopped bracing?"
  },
  "50": {
    "gift": "You regulate the atmosphere around you without trying, and people breathe more easily in your kitchen, your car, your corner of the office. Your own body runs best on order you feel in your gut — clean food, aired rooms, settled accounts, nothing quietly rotting in a drawer or in a friendship. You notice when something has gone off in a household days before anyone names it aloud, a working sense of equilibrium most people never learn to trust in themselves. Your energy holds steady across long stretches whenever your own standards are kept, and that physical steadiness quietly feeds everyone who lives or works beside you.",
    "shadow": "Corruption in this position rarely looks dramatic; it looks like small standards quietly dropped until your body stops feeling clean. You take on responsibility for other people’s wellbeing, absorb their disorder, and find yourself carrying a heaviness in the stomach and shoulders that no amount of sleep clears. The fear underneath is that if you stop holding everything together, the whole thing spoils, so you keep guarding instead of resting. Compromise on food, sleep, or company you know is wrong for you shows up fast in your skin, digestion, and mood. Then you tighten into rules and policing, which drains you further and turns care into a job with no hours off.",
    "invitation": "Where has your body been telling you a standard slipped? Restore one small thing today — the room, the meal, the hour, the boundary — and let the rest wait. You are allowed to put down a burden that was never issued to you. When your own equilibrium comes first, what harmony spreads outward without you managing a single person?"
  },
  "51": {
    "gift": "You rebound fast, and shock that flattens other people acts on your system like a starter motor rather than a brake. Your energy answers challenge directly and physically — cold water, hard training, a real deadline, a risk taken early in the morning while the fear is still fresh. You recover from physical and emotional setbacks at a speed most people around you find genuinely startling, and you rarely give yourself any credit for it. Your initiative is physical before it is mental, so your whole body feels best when you move first and think while moving, with something live and unfinished in front of you.",
    "shadow": "Agitation is the same current with nowhere to discharge, and it lives in your chest and hands. You get restless in calm periods, pick fights with your own schedule, and manufacture urgency so the system has something to push against. Sleep suffers first, then appetite, then patience with the people closest to you, who feel the crackle before you admit to it. The belief driving it is that stillness means falling behind, that a quiet week is evidence something has gone wrong with you. So you take on the fifth thing, run on adrenaline and caffeine, and mistake the shakiness for aliveness until your body forces the stop you refused to schedule.",
    "invitation": "Which direction does your charge take when there is nothing worth charging at? Put it into your body this week — one hard, short, real effort, done early. You are allowed to be bored without treating it as an emergency. When your energy stops needing a crisis to move, what awakening waits on the far side of the adrenaline?"
  },
  "52": {
    "gift": "You quiet a room simply by sitting still inside it, and the people near you slow their breathing without ever knowing why they did. Your restraint is muscular, not moral — you hold energy back until the moment is right, and that stored power is exactly what makes your action land. Your body restores itself through genuine stopping rather than distraction, so one hour of real quiet does more for your system than a whole weekend of pleasant activity. You hold a capacity for stillness that most people chase through years of practice, and you have carried it since childhood without ever calling it anything.",
    "shadow": "Stress in this position is stored, not spent, and it collects in the lower back, the hips, and the jaw. You hold still when you actually want to move, and the held energy hardens into stiffness, clenching, and a sleep that never quite goes deep. The fear underneath is that if you let yourself go, something in you will be too much, so you keep the brake on permanently. Then the pressure builds and comes out sideways — a sudden collapse, a lost week, a body that simply refuses to get up. You also mistake numbness for calm, sitting quietly while nothing in you is actually at rest and the tension keeps climbing.",
    "invitation": "Something your body holds has no reason left to be held? Move it deliberately today, then stop completely for ten minutes with nothing to look at. You are allowed to rest without earning it first. When holding and releasing become the same easy rhythm, what stillness opens underneath the effort?"
  },
  "53": {
    "gift": "You thrive on beginnings, and your body knows the difference between a fresh start and a routine you are only enduring. Your energy rises around new places, new food, new people, new movement, and that appetite for the untried keeps you physically curious for life. You seed things easily and let them grow at their own pace, an unforced expansion most people manufacture instead and then exhaust themselves defending. Your vitality is fed by small daily novelty rather than grand overhaul, which means you stay well on very ordinary resources while other people wait around for a dramatic reset that never comes.",
    "shadow": "Immaturity here shows as a body started too many times and finished too rarely. You begin the training plan, the diet, the sleep schedule, and abandon each one just before it would have paid you back, then read the flatness that follows as proof you lack discipline. Impatience with slow growth pushes you into intensity your system has not been built up for, and injury or burnout arrives on schedule. Underneath runs the fear that you are behind, that everyone else has already grown into something you keep postponing. So you scatter your energy across beginnings, sleep badly on the excitement, and never stay anywhere long enough to feel the strength arrive.",
    "invitation": "Which beginning of yours deserves to become a middle? Choose one physical practice and keep it going for six unremarkable weeks in a row. You are allowed to grow slower than the people around you. When your patience matches your appetite, what superabundance builds in a body that finally stayed put long enough?"
  },
  "54": {
    "gift": "You last, and your body was built for sustained effort rather than sprints, holding output long after other people have quietly stopped. Your energy responds to ambition with a real physical component — training, building, saving, working steadily toward something solid enough to point at and measure. You convert ordinary drive into stamina most people never develop, and you routinely underestimate how much you actually get through on an average, unremarkable Tuesday. Your wellbeing depends on the climb having a meaning attached, and when it does, your sleep deepens, your appetite steadies, and your aspiration carries you upward for years without the strain other people feel.",
    "shadow": "Greed in this position is not about money; it is about never letting the body arrive. You push through tiredness because stopping feels like losing ground, and the fatigue accumulates somewhere you cannot see until it lands all at once. Comparison drives it — someone is further up, so your rest gets postponed again, and you eat, drink, or scroll to blunt the pressure at night. The false belief is that your worth is a function of your ascent, that a resting body is a wasted one. Then the wanting spreads into food, buying, and stimulation, and you end up heavy, wired, and further from the vitality you were climbing toward.",
    "invitation": "What would your body ask for if arriving were allowed? Take one full day this week with no ground gained and nothing improved. You are allowed to want less than you were told to want. When your climbing serves your aliveness rather than draining it, what ascension begins in the body itself?"
  },
  "55": {
    "gift": "You ride your own emotional weather, and your energy moves in waves that are as physical as the tides they resemble. Your high days carry genuine radiance and your low days carry real depth, and you have learned to eat, sleep, and move differently inside each one. You feel the mood of a whole group before anyone speaks, a sensitivity most people would need years of training to approach. Your freedom lives in refusing to fake a state you are not in, and your body rewards that honesty with cleaner sleep, a steadier appetite, and energy that comes back reliably on its own schedule.",
    "shadow": "Victimisation begins the moment you decide your energy is something happening to you rather than something you live with. On a low day you cancel everything, read the flatness as a verdict on your life, and go looking for the person or event to blame it on. Food, drink, and screens become the way you argue with the wave, and each one costs you the next two days as well. The belief underneath is that a good life would feel good continuously, so every dip registers as evidence something is wrong with you. Then you wait to be rescued — by a diagnosis, a partner, a change of circumstance — while the actual rhythm goes unlearned.",
    "invitation": "On a low day with nobody watching, how does your body actually behave? Track your waves for one month, plainly, without arguing with any of them. You are allowed to have a bad week that means nothing. When you stop fighting your own rhythm, what freedom opens in a body that trusts its weather?"
  },
  "56": {
    "gift": "You savour things properly — food, music, warmth, movement — and your body is fed by pleasure in a way most people have talked themselves out of. Your energy climbs around beauty and good company, and a genuinely enjoyed meal does more for your system than any regimen you have forced on yourself. You bring colour into flat rooms simply by arriving, and people leave your company noticeably hungrier for their own lives. Your enrichment is a real physical resource, so when you let yourself be delighted on ordinary days, your vitality holds through long stretches of work that would empty most other people out completely.",
    "shadow": "Distraction is appetite without attention, and it shows up in your body as a night that ends later and emptier than planned. You reach for the next taste, the next screen, the next outing, and the pleasure thins with every repetition while the tiredness thickens underneath. Late eating, late scrolling, and one more drink erode your sleep first, and your mornings pay the bill you keep refusing to look at. The fear running the whole pattern is that stillness would turn out dull, that without constant stimulation there might be nothing much underneath. So you keep the input high, feel constantly busy and quietly unfed, and mistake being entertained for being alive.",
    "invitation": "What actually nourished you last week, as opposed to what merely filled the hours? Take one pleasure and give it your whole attention, slowly, with nothing else running. You are allowed to want less input and more depth. When your delight has your full presence, what intoxication is available in an ordinary evening?"
  },
  "57": {
    "gift": "You read atmospheres through your body first, and your instincts arrive as plain physical fact long before your mind assembles any reason for them. Your health rests on listening to that instrument early — the room you leave, the food you decline, the plan that felt wrong in your stomach on Monday. You hear tone and sound more finely than the people around you, an intuition most treat as ordinary in themselves and never actually possess. Your energy renews in quiet, in natural light, and in clean air, and whenever you protect those conditions your clarity and your stamina rise together.",
    "shadow": "Unease is your intuition arriving with the volume up and nowhere safe to land. You pick up the tension in a house, a partner, or a workplace and hold it in your gut as though it belonged to you, then wonder why you feel ill for no reason. Noise, crowds, and rushed decisions leave you frayed, and you push through anyway because the discomfort seems too vague to justify leaving. The belief underneath is that your sensitivity is a weakness to be managed rather than information to be acted on. So you override the early signal, stay too long, sleep badly on it, and end up with anxiety that no longer points at anything specific.",
    "invitation": "Your body knew something on Monday — what that you argued with by Thursday? Act once this week on a quiet signal you would normally talk yourself out of. You are allowed to leave a room without a reason. When you trust the first whisper, what clarity settles into a body that stopped overriding itself?"
  },
  "58": {
    "gift": "You radiate more raw aliveness than the people around you, and it shows in your face, your pace, and the energy of any room you enter. Your body wants to move, and movement rather than rest alone is what actually recharges you after a heavy or discouraging day. You improve whatever you touch almost automatically, and you rarely notice how much of your group’s shared vitality has been quietly running on yours. Your joy is physical and contagious, so when you are well fed, well slept, and well used, you lift entire households and teams simply by being present in the room with them.",
    "shadow": "Dissatisfaction is this same energy pointed at everything that is not yet good enough, starting with your own body. You scan for the flaw, correct posture and diet and routine endlessly, and never arrive at a version of yourself you would call fine. Overtraining and overworking follow close behind, because the restlessness reads as motivation right up until the injury or the crash makes the argument for you. Underneath sits the belief that being at ease would mean settling, that your standards are the only thing keeping you from decline. You also spend your vitality improving other people’s lives while your own sleep, meals, and pleasures stay unattended and quietly depleted.",
    "invitation": "What in your body is already well, right now, unimproved? Move your body today for enjoyment alone, with nothing at all being fixed or measured. You are allowed to be satisfied before everything is finished. When your energy stops hunting for faults, what bliss is waiting in the ordinary hour you keep skipping?"
  },
  "59": {
    "gift": "You open easily in real closeness, and your body registers safety with another person faster than your reasoning ever could. Your energy is fed by touch, by honest conversation, and by being fully known to a small handful of people rather than pleasantly known to many. You strip the pretence out of a room and let everyone in it breathe out, an ease with intimacy most people spend decades slowly learning to allow. Your sleep, appetite, and mood follow the state of your closest bonds directly, so tending those few relationships is easily the most practical health measure available to you in any given week.",
    "shadow": "Dishonesty here is rarely a lie; it is the small false face you keep on when the truth would cost you closeness. You say you are fine, stay agreeable, and your body files the gap as tension in the throat, the chest, and the stomach. Retreating from intimacy for too long leaves you dry and irritable, and you call it needing space when your system is actually starving. The belief underneath is that being seen entirely would end the relationship, so you offer the acceptable version and stay lonely in company. Sexual and emotional energy either shuts down completely or gets spent somewhere it does not nourish you, and the flatness that follows is physical.",
    "invitation": "Are you being pleasant where honesty was wanted? Say one true, uncomfortable thing to someone close to you this week. You are allowed to be met exactly as you already are. When your closest relationships hold nothing hidden, what transparency arrives in a body that stopped bracing to be liked?"
  },
  "60": {
    "gift": "You work well inside limits, and your body thrives on the kind of structure that would feel like a cage to almost anyone else you know. Your energy comes from rhythm — regular hours, honest portions, familiar routes, a week whose shape you already know and rely on. You see exactly what is actually possible with the time and the strength you have, and that realism keeps you well while other people burn out on optimism. You get far more out of small, consistent effort than most people ever extract from their dramatic overhauls, and that quiet physical efficiency is genuinely rare.",
    "shadow": "Limitation lands in the body as a low ceiling you stopped testing years ago. You decide what your energy cannot do, arrange life around that verdict, and slowly narrow your movement, your food, and your world until the smallness feels like fact. Frustration builds against the constraints anyway, and it settles into your joints, your neck, and a persistent tiredness that ordinary rest never quite touches. The belief underneath is that the restriction is permanent and personal, that your body is simply the one that missed out. Then you either force through it and injure yourself, or give up entirely and lose the rhythm that was actually keeping you well.",
    "invitation": "Which of your limits have you tested this year, and which have you only assumed? Push gently on one of them this week and watch what your body actually reports. You are allowed to outgrow a rule that once protected you. When your structure serves your life, what justice does your body finally receive?"
  },
  "61": {
    "gift": "You take energy directly from silence, and one hour alone at dawn does more for your system than a full night of good company ever will. Your body responds to solitude, open sky, and quiet the way other people respond to holidays, and it recovers there completely and quickly. You hold a stillness in your presence that calms others without a word, something most people would need long practice to counterfeit. Your inspiration arrives physically — a lift in the chest, a lightness, sudden clean energy — and whenever you build your days to allow it, your health follows that current reliably.",
    "shadow": "Psychosis in this position is the mind spinning faster than the body can carry, usually at three in the morning. You get gripped by an idea, stop eating properly, stop sleeping, and run on a strange bright energy that feels like vision until it collapses. Too much solitude tips into isolation, and without ordinary company your thinking loses its edges and your body loses its routine. The fear underneath is that the insight will vanish if you stop chasing it, so rest looks like a betrayal of something sacred. You also lose touch with hunger, warmth, and tiredness entirely, and only notice the neglect when your system finally refuses to continue.",
    "invitation": "Ask what your body needs when your mind runs hottest? Ground one revelation this week in something plain — a cooked meal, a long walk, a full night’s sleep. You are allowed to come back down without losing the insight. When your inspiration lands in your ordinary flesh, what sanctity shows up in an ordinary morning?"
  },
  "62": {
    "gift": "You track detail effortlessly, and your body responds well to a life you have genuinely organised — meals planned, sleep timed, small messes dealt with early. Your energy holds when your environment is precise, because clutter and vagueness cost you real physical stamina that most people never account for. You explain complicated things clearly and calmly under pressure, and you have little idea how much strain that spares everyone listening to you. Your precision reaches your own body too, so you catch small changes in your health early enough to act on them, and that noticing protects you year after year.",
    "shadow": "Intellect here means living from the neck up while the rest of you waits outside the conversation. You analyse your tiredness rather than sleeping, research the diet rather than eating, and describe your body accurately while ignoring what it is asking for. Mental overwork tightens the eyes, neck, and shoulders, and the thinking keeps running long after the light is off, so the sleep you get is thin. The belief driving it is that understanding a problem is nearly the same as solving it, which leaves your body permanently one step behind your mind. Then perfectionism about small details drains the energy that a straightforward, imperfect meal and an early night would have restored.",
    "invitation": "Your body has been talking while you analysed it — saying what while you were busy analysing it? Do one plain physical thing today without researching any part of it first. You are allowed to act before you fully understand. When your precision serves your living rather than replacing it, what impeccability appears in the way you actually feel?"
  },
  "63": {
    "gift": "You question everything, and that same searching energy keeps you paying genuine attention to what your body reports back to you each day. Your vitality answers genuine inquiry — you test a change, watch honestly, and keep only what works, rather than following whatever health advice happened to be loudest that year. You spot the flaw in a plan before anyone else does, and you consistently mistake that for scepticism instead of the accuracy it actually is. Your energy runs cleanest when your mind has a worthwhile problem, and your sleep deepens as soon as the question you are carrying has somewhere useful to go.",
    "shadow": "Doubt in this position keeps the mind switched on when your body is trying to shut down for the night. You lie awake reviewing what you got wrong, run the same scene again, and wake unrested with the thinking already in progress. Turned inward, doubt attacks your own health — nothing you do feels sufficient, every symptom gets a worst case, and reassurance never quite settles you. The fear underneath is that certainty is unsafe, that if you stopped checking, something would slip past and cost you. So you second-guess the food, the training, and the doctor in turn, and the constant questioning drains more energy than any of the choices ever would.",
    "invitation": "Which question do you keep re-checking that was answered months ago? Give your questions a set hour and keep the rest of the day for your body. You are allowed to stop investigating and go to sleep. When your inquiry points outward again, what truth becomes livable in a rested nervous system?"
  },
  "64": {
    "gift": "You dream vividly, and your nights carry as much activity as your days do, which means genuine rest matters more for you than for almost anyone you know. Your imagination fills the quiet moments with images, and your energy climbs whenever you have somewhere real to put them — music, drawing, writing, building, cooking. You hold enormous amounts of unfinished material lightly, and you badly underrate how unusual it is to live comfortably with that much going on inside. Your body settles beautifully once the mental pictures find an outlet, and your sleep, your appetite, and your mood improve together whenever they do.",
    "shadow": "Confusion here is mental traffic with no exit, and it lands in the body as a headache, a foggy morning, and a strange bone tiredness. You wake with fragments you cannot order, try to make sense of them before breakfast, and start the day already behind your own mind. Screens late at night feed the machinery directly, so the images multiply, sleep breaks up, and the next day is spent chasing coherence you were never going to find. The belief underneath is that the confusion means something is wrong, when it is only unprocessed material waiting for form. Then you push for clarity, exhaust yourself, and mistake the resulting flatness for a problem with your health.",
    "invitation": "What are your dreams handing you that you keep trying to solve? Put one image into a made thing this week without explaining it. You are allowed to be confused for a while without fixing it. When your inner pictures have a place to land, what illumination comes to a body that finally slept?"
  }
});
